 "texts": [
  {
   "profile": "J1",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## What photosynthesis does\n- Plants make their own food through photosynthesis.\n- Carbon dioxide enters through leaf openings; water enters through roots.\n- Light energy turns CO₂ and water into glucose and oxygen.\n\n## Where it happens\n- Leaves are the main site of photosynthesis.\n- Chloroplasts inside leaf cells contain chlorophyll.\n- Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light; reflects green.\n\n## Outputs and balance\n- Oxygen leaves through leaf openings; animals and people breathe it in.\n- Word equation: carbon dioxide + water --light--> glucose + oxygen.\n- Low light, water, or CO₂ slows glucose production and growth.",
    "mirror": "## What Photosynthesis Is\n- Photosynthesis is the process plants use to make their own food.\n- A plant takes in carbon dioxide from the air through tiny openings in its leaves and takes in water from the soil through its roots.\n- Using light energy from the sun, the plant turns carbon dioxide and water into glucose, a kind of sugar.\n- The glucose is the plant's food, and the plant uses it to grow and to store energy.\n\n## Where It Happens and the Green Pigment\n- Photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves.\n- Inside the cells of a leaf are small green structures called chloroplasts.\n- They contain a green pigment called chlorophyll.\n- Chlorophyll absorbs light energy from the sun, mostly red and blue light, and reflects green light, which is why leaves look green.\n- Without chlorophyll the plant could not capture the light energy it needs.\n- Think of chlorophyll like a solar panel on a rooftop; it catches sunlight so the plant can use that energy.\n\n## Oxygen and the Word Equation\n- Oxygen is released as a by-product of photosynthesis.\n- The oxygen leaves the plant through the same tiny openings in the leaves.\n- Animals and people breathe in this oxygen, which is one reason plants matter so much to life on Earth.\n- The whole process can be written as a word equation: carbon dioxide plus water, using light energy, makes glucose plus oxygen.\n- Think of the word equation like a comic panel sequence: first the ingredients arrive, then sunlight powers the change, and finally the products appear.\n\n## What Slows the Process\n- A plant that gets too little light, too little water or too little carbon dioxide makes less glucose and grows more slowly.\n- This is why a plant kept in a dark cupboard turns pale and weak.\n- Layout: Light + CO2 + H2O -> Glucose + O2\n- Quick check: What word equation sums up photosynthesis? Answer: carbon dioxide plus water, using light energy, makes glucose plus oxygen."
   }
  },
  {
   "profile": "J3",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Militarism\n- Historians often describe four long-term causes of the First World War, plus one event that set it off.\n- The first is militarism.\n- In the years before 1914 the great powers built up large armies and navies, and Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Generals began to plan for war, and many leaders came to see it as a normal way to settle disputes.\n- Generals began to plan for war, and many leaders came to see it as a normal way to settle disputes.\n\n## Alliances\n- The second cause is alliances.\n- Europe was divided into two blocs.\n- The Triple Entente joined France, Russia and Britain, and the Triple Alliance joined Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy.\n- Because of these alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries, so a quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n- Think of it like a multiplayer video game where two rival clans have mutual defence pacts, so a skirmish between two players instantly drags both full rosters into an all-out clan war.\n\n## Imperialism\n- The third cause is imperialism, which means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- The European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia, which increased distrust between them.\n\n## Nationalism\n- The fourth cause is nationalism, strong pride in one's nation or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans, where Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples and Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n\n## The Spark: Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n- Because of the alliances, Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia soon drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain.\n- How did the alliance system turn a regional assassination into a world war?",
    "mirror": "## Four long-term causes plus a spark\n- Historians identify four long-term causes of WWI and one spark event.\n- Think of it like a game lobby where four settings load before the match starts.\n\n## Militarism\n- Militarism means great powers built large armies and navies before 1914.\n- Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Generals made war plans and leaders saw war as normal.\n- Think of it like an arms race in a strategy game where every faction maxes out units before the first turn.\n- Layout: Militarism -> large armies/navies -> naval race (Germany vs Britain) -> war plans -> war seen as normal.\n\n## Alliances\n- Europe split into two alliance blocs.\n- The Triple Entente joined France, Russia and Britain.\n- The Triple Alliance joined Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy.\n- Alliances let a local quarrel pull in many countries.\n- Think of it like a party chat where one argument drags the whole squad into a raid.\n- Layout: Two blocs -> Triple Entente (France, Russia, Britain) and Triple Alliance (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy) -> local conflict spreads.\n\n## Imperialism\n- Imperialism means rivalry for colonies, markets and power.\n- European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia.\n- This competition increased distrust between them.\n- Think of it like a comic map where every faction grabs territory and the tension rises panel by panel.\n\n## Nationalism\n- Nationalism is strong pride in one's nation or a wish for independence.\n- It was especially powerful in the Balkans.\n- Serbia wanted to unite Slavic peoples.\n- Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n- Think of it like a guild where one clan pushes for unity and the big clan fears a split.\n\n## The spark: assassination\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n- He was heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne.\n- Alliances turned Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia into a wider war.\n- Think of it like a single headshot that triggers a full server war because of locked-in teams.\n- Layout: Assassination (28 June 1914) -> Austria-Hungary vs Serbia -> alliances pull in Russia, Germany, France, Britain."
   }
  },
  {
   "profile": "J4",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Greetings\n- Picture this: you say hola and it works morning, noon or night with anyone.\n- Step 1: pick buenos días for morning, buenas tardes for afternoon, buenas noches for evening.\n- Layout: hola → time phrases → adiós / hasta luego → how are you → mucho gusto.\n- The simplest is hola, which means hello. You can use it at any time of day\n\n## Goodbyes\n- Next, step 2: say adiós for a plain goodbye or hasta luego when you will meet again soon.\n- Hasta luego is friendly and expects another meeting soon.\n\n## Asking how someone is\n- Then, step 3: use the informal cómo estás with friends, family or children.\n- Step 4: use the formal cómo está usted with older people, strangers or in polite situations.\n- The usual reply is bien, gracias meaning fine, thank you.\n- Think of it like a basketball huddle: informal for teammates you know, formal for the referee you respect.\n- Think of it like drawing a comic panel: casual speech bubble for friends, polite caption for strangers.\n\n## Meeting someone new\n- Finally, step 5: say mucho gusto when you meet someone for the first time.\n- Mucho gusto means nice to meet you.",
    "mirror": "## Basic greetings and time‑specific phrases\n- Hola means hello and can be used at any time with friends or new acquaintances.\n- Buenos días means good morning, buenas tardes means good afternoon, and buenas noches means good evening or good night.\n- Días is masculine plural while tardes and noches are feminine plural, so the adjective changes from buenos to buenas.\n\n## Goodbyes\n- Adiós means goodbye and works in any situation.\n- Hasta luego means see you later and is friendly when you expect to see the person again soon.\n\n## Asking how someone is\n- With friends, family and children use the informal cómo estás for how are you.\n- With older people, strangers or in polite situations use the formal cómo está usted, also meaning how are you.\n- The usual answer is bien, gracias, which means fine, thank you.\n\n## First meeting\n- When you meet someone for the first time, say mucho gusto, which means nice to meet you."
   }
  },
  {
   "profile": "J2",
   "kind": "notes"
  }
 ],
